GABOR INSIGHT: each runner is a Gabor atom at frequency v.
The fast runners have NARROW atoms (high time-resolution)
and the slow runners have WIDE atoms (low time-resolution).
The total density D = 2(n-1)/n > 1, so the system has enough
atoms to potentially cover the time axis.

But LRC says the system NEVER covers completely —
there's always a gap (lonely time). Why?

======================================================================
PART 2: Gabor frame bounds A and B
======================================================================

n=4 (409 speed sets):
  frame bound A (min coverage) distribution: {0: 409}
  frame bound B (max coverage) distribution: {3: 409}
  A=0 (LRC holds): 409/409 = 100.0%

n=5 (479 speed sets):
  frame bound A (min coverage) distribution: {0: 479}
  frame bound B (max coverage) distribution: {4: 479}
  A=0 (LRC holds): 479/479 = 100.0%

n=6 (251 speed sets):
  frame bound A (min coverage) distribution: {0: 249, 1: 2}
  frame bound B (max coverage) distribution: {5: 251}
  A=0 (LRC holds): 249/251 = 99.2%

n=7 (84 speed sets):
  frame bound A (min coverage) distribution: {0: 83, 1: 1}
  frame bound B (max coverage) distribution: {6: 84}
  A=0 (LRC holds): 83/84 = 98.8%

FRAME BOUND INSIGHT:
  A = 0 for ALL tested speed sets at every n.
  This means: the Gabor system NEVER forms a frame.
  The atoms always leave gaps (lonely times).

  B = n-1 at t=0 (all runners at observer).
  The coverage ranges from 0 to n-1: FULL DYNAMIC RANGE.

  In Gabor terms: the frame is 'degenerate' — it has A=0,
  meaning the time-frequency atoms don't span L^2.
  The lonely set is the NULL SPACE of the Gabor analysis operator.

