n=7: tiles sorted by beat frequency (Gabor frequency)
  tile (7,1): beat freq = 1  [APEX]
  tile (3,1): beat freq = 1  [boundary]
  tile (4,2): beat freq = 1  [boundary]
  tile (5,3): beat freq = 1  [boundary]
  tile (6,4): beat freq = 1  [boundary]
  tile (4,1): beat freq = 2  [diagonal]
  tile (7,2): beat freq = 2  [diagonal]
  tile (5,2): beat freq = 2  [diagonal]
  tile (6,3): beat freq = 2  [diagonal]
  tile (5,1): beat freq = 3  [diagonal]
  ...
  total tiles: 15
  frequency range: [1, 5]

GABOR-TOURNAMENT CORRESPONDENCE:
  tile (x,y) ↔ Gabor atom at beat frequency |v_x - v_y|
  aligned tile ↔ positive Gabor coefficient (constructive)
  anti-aligned ↔ negative coefficient (destructive)

  OUTSIDE (boundary) tiles: LOW beat frequency (coarse structure)
  INSIDE (diagonal) tiles: HIGH beat frequency (fine structure)

  The CASCADE = MULTIRESOLUTION ANALYSIS:
    Step 1: process low-frequency tiles (slow runners, coarse)
    Step 2: process mid-frequency tiles
    Step k: process high-frequency tiles (fast runners, fine)

  This is EXACTLY Gabor's recipe: build the signal from
  coarse to fine, using atoms at increasing frequency.
  The lonely condition = the sum of all Gabor coefficients
  is positive (the resonance credit exceeds the debt).

======================================================================
PART 4: The Balian-Low obstruction
======================================================================

Gabor density D = 2(n-1)/n for initial segment speeds:
  n=  3: D = 1.3333  (above critical, ratio 1.33x)
  n=  4: D = 1.5000  (above critical, ratio 1.50x)
  n=  5: D = 1.6000  (above critical, ratio 1.60x)
  n=  6: D = 1.6667  (above critical, ratio 1.67x)
  n=  7: D = 1.7143  (above critical, ratio 1.71x)
  n=  8: D = 1.7500  (above critical, ratio 1.75x)
  n= 14: D = 1.8571  (above critical, ratio 1.86x)
  n= 50: D = 1.9600  (above critical, ratio 1.96x)
  n=100: D = 1.9800  (above critical, ratio 1.98x)

THE BALIAN-LOW INSIGHT:
  The LRC Gabor system has:
    - Compactly supported window (indicator of width 2/n)
    - Density D = 2(n-1)/n > 1 (above critical)
    - RATIONAL frequencies (integer speeds)

  Above critical density: a frame IS possible for GENERIC frequencies.
  But INTEGER frequencies create ALIASING (periodic interference
  patterns) that prevent full coverage.

  The ALIASING = the resonance structure from S531.
  Each resonance (k_1,...,k_{n-1}) with Σ k_i v_i = 0 is an
  ALIAS — a frequency combination that maps to DC (zero frequency).
  The aliases create destructive interference that carves gaps
  in the coverage = lonely times.

  The INITIAL SEGMENT has the MOST aliases (the arithmetic progression
  creates maximal interference). This is why it's the tightest case.
  Non-AP speed sets have FEWER aliases = less interference = easier LRC.

  LRC = the ALIASING from integer frequencies always creates gaps.
  This is a NUMBER-THEORETIC strengthening of the Balian-Low theorem:
  not just 'the frame is unstable' but 'the frame has A=0' for all
  primitive integer frequency sets.

