SPECTROGRAM INSIGHT:
  Each column = one runner's 'shadow' (close zone pattern)
  Each row = one time instant's coverage
  Lonely = an entire row of dots (no shadows)

  The PATTERN: fast runners (high v) have thin, frequent shadows.
  Slow runners (low v) have wide, rare shadows.
  The lonely times are in the GAPS between shadow clusters.

  In GABOR terms: the spectrogram IS the Gabor transform.
  Each shadow = a Gabor atom. The lonely time = a point in
  the time-frequency plane where NO atom is present.
  LRC = the Gabor system has a non-trivial NULL SPACE.

======================================================================
GRAND SYNTHESIS: Gabor × Tournament = LRC
======================================================================

THE CORRESPONDENCE:
  Runner v_i          ↔  Gabor atom at frequency v_i
  Close zone [0,2/n]  ↔  Window function of width 2/n
  Tournament tile     ↔  Gabor coefficient (beat frequency)
  Resonance order r   ↔  r-point correlation in TF plane
  Inside/outside      ↔  High/low frequency
  Cascade             ↔  Multiresolution analysis
  Lonely time         ↔  Null of the Gabor analysis operator
  Resonance debt      ↔  Aliasing from integer frequencies

WHY LRC IS TRUE (Gabor perspective):
  1. The Gabor density D = 2(n-1)/n < 2 is INSUFFICIENT to
     cover the time axis with compactly-supported atoms.
  2. Even if D were sufficient: INTEGER frequencies create ALIASING
     that carves gaps in the coverage (the resonance structure).
  3. The INITIAL SEGMENT has maximal aliasing (AP = maximal coherence)
     but even then, the coverage has gaps (wall-only lonely times).
  4. Non-AP speeds have LESS aliasing → MORE gaps → easier LRC.

THE FORMAL GROUP F(x,y)=(x+y)/(1+xy) IS the GABOR COMPOSITION LAW:
  It adds dissonances (distances from observer) hyperbolically.
  The rapidity = log of the Gabor atom's time-frequency area.
  The lonely threshold 0.5·ln(n-1) = the minimum atom area
  for a single runner to be 'resolved' (safe from observer).

